WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.112
Post-quantum cryptography is not only a research topic.

00:00:03.112 --> 00:00:07.631
It is a practical migration issue for any organization that relies on

00:00:07.631 --> 00:00:09.514
public-key cryptography for key exchange,

00:00:09.514 --> 00:00:12.150
digital signatures, certificates, software updates, service authentication,

00:00:12.150 --> 00:00:13.279
or long-lived confidentiality.

00:00:13.279 --> 00:00:17.798
Quantum-capable attacks are not something most security teams can observe directly today,

00:00:17.798 --> 00:00:22.317
but migration work has to start before systems are already under pressure.

00:00:22.317 --> 00:00:26.459
NIST standards matter because they give vendors, governments, architects, developers, auditors,

00:00:26.459 --> 00:00:29.094
and security teams a shared reference point.

00:00:29.094 --> 00:00:29.848
Without standards,

00:00:29.848 --> 00:00:34.366
every organization would have to interpret the algorithm landscape on its own.

00:00:34.366 --> 00:00:38.132
Standards reduce uncertainty by naming the algorithms, parameter sets, interfaces,

00:00:38.132 --> 00:00:44.157
and expected security categories that can be used as the basis for products and migration planning.

00:00:44.157 --> 00:00:48.299
It helps to separate several stages that are often blended together.

00:00:48.299 --> 00:00:52.441
Algorithm selection means NIST has chosen an algorithm family for standardization.

00:00:52.441 --> 00:00:55.453
Standardization means the formal standard has been published.

00:00:55.453 --> 00:00:58.466
Implementation means libraries and products actually support it.

00:00:58.466 --> 00:01:02.231
Validation means an implementation can be tested through recognized programs.

00:01:02.231 --> 00:01:06.750
Deployment means real systems have adopted it safely, interoperably, and at scale.

00:01:06.750 --> 00:01:11.645
Security practitioners do not need to become cryptographers to use the standards well.

00:01:11.645 --> 00:01:15.410
They do need to understand what each standard is for,

00:01:15.410 --> 00:01:20.306
where it may appear in real systems, and what migration questions it raises.

00:01:20.306 --> 00:01:25.201
The practical job is to connect standards to inventory, vendor readiness, protocol choices,

00:01:25.201 --> 00:01:27.460
testing, key management, and staged rollout.
